> Vaccines work by preparing the immune system to recognize and fight pathogens, such as viruses and bacteria, without causing the disease itself. They introduce a harmless part of the pathogen, like a protein or a dead version, prompting the body to produce antibodies. If the real pathogen enters the body later, the immune system recognizes it and quickly dispatches the invader, often preventing illness. This process can also create a "memory" for the immune system, allowing it to respond faster and more effectively if exposed to the actual pathogen in the future. Vaccines are a crucial tool in preventing diseases and saving lives.

